// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 8): space.
ADDART({id:"europaclipper",t:"Europa Clipper",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Europa Clipper",r:["europa","jupiter","astrobio","enceladus","junoprobe"],
p:[["Mission to an ocean moon","Europa Clipper will study Jupiter’s moon Europa, which hides an ocean under its ice."],["NASA’s biggest planetary probe","It is the largest spacecraft NASA has built for a planetary mission."],["Arriving in 2030","After a six-year journey, it will reach Jupiter in April 2030."]],
l:"Europa Clipper is a NASA spacecraft on its way to study Europa, one of Jupiter’s four large moons, which scientists believe has a salty ocean of liquid water beneath its icy crust containing more water than all of Earth’s oceans. The mission’s goal is to determine whether Europa could support life by studying its ice shell, ocean, composition and geology. Launched on a SpaceX Falcon Heavy rocket on 14 October 2024, Europa Clipper is the largest spacecraft NASA has ever built for a planetary mission, with solar arrays spanning about 30 meters to collect faint sunlight at Jupiter. It flew past Mars in March 2025 and is due to swing past Earth in December 2026 to gain speed before arriving at Jupiter in April 2030. Rather than orbiting Europa, which lies in Jupiter’s intense radiation belts, it will orbit Jupiter and make about 49 close flybys of Europa, some as low as 25 kilometers above the surface, using nine science instruments including cameras, ice-penetrating radar and a mass spectrometer to sample particles. The European Space Agency’s Juice spacecraft will also study Jupiter’s icy moons at the same time.",
s:[["Why Europa?","Europa’s surface is crisscrossed with cracks and ridges, suggesting moving ice over an ocean. Jupiter’s gravity flexes the moon, heating its interior. Water, chemical ingredients and energy, three needs for life as we know it, may all be present. The Hubble Space Telescope has seen possible plumes of water vapor."],["Surviving radiation","Jupiter’s radiation could fry electronics. Clipper’s sensitive electronics are protected inside a vault with walls of titanium and aluminum. Each flyby is quick, and the spacecraft spends most of its time farther from Jupiter."],["Instruments","Instruments include cameras to map the surface, a thermal imager to find warm spots, radar to probe the ice shell for water pockets, a magnetometer to measure the ocean’s depth and saltiness, and dust and gas analyzers to sample particles knocked off the surface or possible plumes."],["A message on board","The spacecraft carries a plate engraved with ‘In Praise of Mystery: A Poem for Europa’ by US Poet Laureate Ada Limón, along with the names of more than 2.6 million people and the word ‘water’ in 103 languages."]],
k:[["Target","Jupiter’s moon Europa"],["Launch","14 October 2024 (Falcon Heavy)"],["Arrival at Jupiter","April 2030"],["Planned flybys","About 49"],["Solar array span","About 30 m"]],
d:[["1979","Voyager images reveal Europa’s cracked ice"],["1995–2003","Galileo spacecraft finds evidence of an ocean"],["2015","NASA approves Europa Clipper"],["2024","Europa Clipper launches"],["2025","Mars flyby"],["2026","Earth flyby (December)"],["2030","Arrival at Jupiter"]],
s2:[["Juice","ESA’s Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer, Juice, launched in April 2023, will arrive at Jupiter in 2031 and eventually orbit Ganymede, the largest moon in the solar system. Scientists plan to coordinate observations between the two missions."],["Could we find life?","Europa Clipper cannot detect life directly, but it will look for conditions that could support it. A future lander might search for signs of life in Europa’s ice."]],
sp:["A huge spacecraft","With its solar arrays unfolded, Europa Clipper is about as wide as a basketball court. It weighed about 6,000 kilograms at launch, more than half of it propellant."],
g:[["Flyby","Passing close to an object without orbiting it"],["Ice shell","The frozen outer layer of an icy moon"],["Plume","A jet of gas or vapor"],["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change speed"]],
f:["Europa may have more water than Earth’s oceans.","Europa Clipper will fly past Europa about 49 times.","It carries a poem for Europa."],
fun:"Europa Clipper’s ice-penetrating radar may be able to see up to about 30 kilometers into Europa’s icy crust."});

ADDART({id:"psyche",t:"Psyche: The Metal Asteroid",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⚙️",w:"Psyche (spacecraft)",r:["asteroidbelt","ionengines","vesta","pulsar","mars"],
p:[["A metal world","The asteroid Psyche may be the exposed core of an early planet."],["NASA’s Psyche mission","A NASA spacecraft is traveling there, arriving in 2029."],["Lasers in space","Psyche tested laser communication across deep space."]],
l:"Psyche is both a large, unusual asteroid and the NASA spacecraft sent to explore it. The asteroid 16 Psyche, discovered by Italian astronomer Annibale de Gasparis on 17 March 1852, orbits in the outer asteroid belt and is about 280 kilometers across at its widest. Observations from Earth suggest that it contains a large amount of metal, iron and nickel, perhaps mixed with rock, leading scientists to suspect it could be part of the core of a planetesimal, an early building block of planets, whose outer layers were stripped away by collisions. Because no one can visit Earth’s core, Psyche offers a unique chance to study what planetary cores are like. The Psyche spacecraft launched on a Falcon Heavy rocket on 13 October 2023 and uses solar-powered Hall-effect thrusters. It flew past Mars on 15 May 2026, gaining speed, and is due to arrive at Psyche in August 2029 to orbit for about two years, mapping its surface, magnetic field and composition. The spacecraft also carried an experiment that sent laser signals from deep space to Earth.",
s:[["What is Psyche made of?","Radar and telescope observations show Psyche is denser and more reflective in radar than most asteroids, suggesting metal. Its density, however, suggests it may also contain rock or be porous. Scientists will find out only when the spacecraft arrives."],["The spacecraft","About the size of a small van with solar arrays spanning about 25 meters, Psyche carries a multispectral imager, a gamma-ray and neutron spectrometer to measure its elements, and a magnetometer to detect any ancient magnetic field, which would support the core theory."],["Laser communication","Psyche carried NASA’s Deep Space Optical Communications experiment, which in 2023 and 2024 beamed high-definition video and data to Earth by laser from millions of kilometers away, including a video of a cat named Taters, showing that lasers could greatly increase data rates for future missions."],["The ‘quadrillion-dollar’ myth","News stories often claim that Psyche’s metals are worth quadrillions of dollars. Scientists say such figures are meaningless, as there is no way to mine or bring the metal back, and flooding markets would make it worthless."]],
k:[["Asteroid size","About 280 km across"],["Discovered","17 March 1852"],["Launch","13 October 2023"],["Mars flyby","15 May 2026"],["Arrival","August 2029"]],
d:[["1852","Annibale de Gasparis discovers Psyche"],["2017","NASA selects the Psyche mission"],["2022","Launch delayed by software problems"],["2023","Psyche launches"],["2026","Mars gravity assist"],["2029","Planned arrival at Psyche"]],
s2:[["A delayed launch","Psyche was meant to launch in 2022, but problems testing its flight software caused a delay. An independent review found management and staffing problems at NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory, leading to changes."],["Named after a myth","The asteroid is named after Psyche, a figure in Greek mythology who represents the soul and married Eros, the god of love."]],
sp:["Lindy Elkins-Tanton","Planetary scientist Lindy Elkins-Tanton of Arizona State University leads the Psyche mission, one of the few NASA planetary missions led by a woman."],
g:[["Planetesimal","An early building block of planets"],["Core","The central part of a planet"],["Hall-effect thruster","A type of electric engine"],["Spectrometer","An instrument that analyzes light or radiation"]],
f:["Psyche may be part of an ancient planet’s core.","The spacecraft flew past Mars in May 2026.","Psyche beamed a cat video to Earth by laser."],
fun:"The first high-definition video sent by laser from deep space showed a cat named Taters chasing a laser pointer."});

ADDART({id:"hayabusa",t:"Hayabusa and Hayabusa2",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🦅",w:"Hayabusa2",r:["asteroidbelt","ionengines","japancountry","spaceagencies","osirisrex"],
p:[["Japan’s asteroid samplers","Japan’s Hayabusa probes brought pieces of asteroids back to Earth."],["A dramatic comeback","The first Hayabusa returned in 2010 after many problems."],["Ryugu samples","Hayabusa2 returned samples from asteroid Ryugu in 2020."]],
l:"Hayabusa and Hayabusa2, named after the Japanese word for peregrine falcon, are robotic spacecraft of the Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency (JAXA) that made the first sample returns from asteroids. Hayabusa launched in 2003 and reached the small asteroid Itokawa in 2005. Despite a failed mini-lander, broken reaction wheels, a fuel leak, loss of contact for weeks and ion engine failures, engineers nursed it home; on 13 June 2010 its capsule landed in Australia with about 1,500 tiny grains of Itokawa, the first asteroid material ever returned. Hayabusa2 launched on 3 December 2014 and reached the carbon-rich asteroid Ryugu in June 2018. It released small hopping rovers, fired a copper projectile to blast a crater and touched down twice to collect samples, returning a capsule to Australia on 6 December 2020 with 5.4 grams of material, including organic molecules, amino acids and uracil, a component of RNA. Hayabusa2 continued on an extended mission, flying past the asteroid Torifune in July 2026 and heading for 1998 KY26 in 2031.",
s:[["Hayabusa’s troubles","After landing on Itokawa in 2005, Hayabusa’s thrusters leaked, it tumbled and contact was lost for seven weeks. Engineers recovered it and, when its ion engines failed, cross-wired two engines to make one working combination. The spacecraft burned up on reentry as planned while its capsule parachuted down."],["Hayabusa2 at Ryugu","Ryugu turned out to be a diamond-shaped rubble pile covered with boulders, making landing difficult. In 2019, Hayabusa2 fired a 2-kilogram copper impactor to create an artificial crater and later collected material from beneath the surface, the first subsurface sample from an asteroid."],["What the samples showed","Ryugu samples are among the most primitive materials studied, similar to rare meteorites. They contain water-bearing minerals and organic compounds, supporting the idea that asteroids may have delivered water and ingredients for life to the early Earth."],["The extended mission","After dropping off its capsule, Hayabusa2 headed out again. It flew past asteroid 2001 CC21, named Torifune, in July 2026 and is due to rendezvous with 1998 KY26, a tiny, fast-spinning asteroid, in 2031."]],
k:[["Agency","JAXA"],["Hayabusa target","Itokawa (returned 2010)"],["Hayabusa2 target","Ryugu (returned 2020)"],["Ryugu sample","5.4 grams"],["Next target","1998 KY26 (2031)"]],
d:[["2003","Hayabusa launches"],["2005","Hayabusa reaches Itokawa"],["2010","Hayabusa returns Itokawa grains"],["2014","Hayabusa2 launches"],["2019","Hayabusa2 blasts a crater on Ryugu"],["2020","Ryugu samples land in Australia"],["2026","Hayabusa2 flies past Torifune"]],
s2:[["A national hero","The first Hayabusa’s survival made it a celebrity in Japan, inspiring several feature films and a planetarium show. People saw its return as a story of perseverance."],["Sharing samples","JAXA shared Ryugu samples with NASA, which in return gave JAXA part of the asteroid Bennu sample from OSIRIS-REx."]],
sp:["MINERVA rovers","Hayabusa2 deployed two tiny MINERVA-II1 rovers on Ryugu in 2018, the first rovers to move on an asteroid. Because gravity was so weak, they moved by hopping."],
g:[["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"],["Rubble pile","An asteroid made of loose rocks held by gravity"],["Organic molecule","A carbon-based molecule"],["Reaction wheel","A wheel used to point a spacecraft"]],
f:["Hayabusa made the first asteroid sample return.","Hayabusa2 blasted a crater on Ryugu.","Ryugu samples contain amino acids."],
fun:"The tiny MINERVA rovers on Ryugu moved by hopping, because a single step could have launched them off into space in its weak gravity."});

ADDART({id:"osirisrex",t:"OSIRIS-REx",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☄️",w:"OSIRIS-REx",r:["asteroidbelt","hayabusa","planetdefense","astrobio","dna"],
p:[["Bringing back Bennu","OSIRIS-REx collected a sample from asteroid Bennu and returned it in 2023."],["Ingredients for life","The sample contains amino acids and all five bases of DNA and RNA."],["Off to Apophis","Renamed OSIRIS-APEX, it will visit asteroid Apophis in 2029."]],
l:"OSIRIS-REx was a NASA mission that collected a sample from the near-Earth asteroid Bennu and returned it to Earth, the first US asteroid sample return. Its name stands for Origins, Spectral Interpretation, Resource Identification and Security–Regolith Explorer. Launched on 8 September 2016, it reached Bennu, a carbon-rich rubble-pile asteroid about 500 meters wide, in December 2018 and mapped it in detail. On 20 October 2020, the spacecraft briefly touched the surface with its robotic arm in a ‘touch-and-go’ maneuver, firing nitrogen gas to stir up rocks and dust; it sank surprisingly deep, revealing that Bennu’s surface is loosely packed like a ball pit. The sample capsule landed in the Utah desert on 24 September 2023 with about 121.6 grams of material, the largest asteroid sample ever returned. Studies published in 2025 found that it contains 14 of the 20 amino acids used by life on Earth, all five nucleobases found in DNA and RNA, and salts left by evaporating brine. The spacecraft, renamed OSIRIS-APEX, is heading for the asteroid Apophis, arriving in 2029.",
s:[["Bennu","Bennu is about 4.5 billion years old and may contain material from the birth of the solar system. It also has a small chance, about 1 in 2,700, of hitting Earth in the late 2100s, which made studying it useful for planetary defense. OSIRIS-REx precisely measured its orbit."],["Touch-and-go","During the sampling maneuver, the arm’s head pushed about half a meter into Bennu’s surface, collecting so much material that some leaked out through a flap jammed open by rocks. Engineers quickly stowed the sample to avoid losing more."],["Opening the capsule","Scientists at NASA’s Johnson Space Center took months to open the sample container fully, because two stubborn fasteners would not unscrew until special tools were made. Samples are shared with researchers around the world."],["Findings","The Bennu sample shows that its parent body once had liquid salty water and contained many ingredients important for life. Scientists stress that it contains no signs of life itself, but supports the idea that asteroids delivered these ingredients to early Earth."]],
k:[["Target","Asteroid Bennu"],["Launch","8 September 2016"],["Sample collected","20 October 2020"],["Sample landed","24 September 2023, Utah"],["Sample mass","About 121.6 g"]],
d:[["2016","OSIRIS-REx launches"],["2018","Arrives at Bennu"],["2020","Collects its sample"],["2023","Sample capsule lands in Utah"],["2025","Studies report amino acids and nucleobases"],["2029","OSIRIS-APEX to reach Apophis"]],
s2:[["OSIRIS-APEX","OSIRIS-APEX will reach Apophis shortly after the asteroid’s close pass of Earth on 13 April 2029, studying how Earth’s gravity changed its surface and spin."],["A Japanese partner","OSIRIS-REx and Japan’s Hayabusa2 studied similar carbon-rich asteroids at the same time, and the two agencies exchanged portions of their samples."]],
sp:["Dante Lauretta","Planetary scientist Dante Lauretta of the University of Arizona led OSIRIS-REx for nearly two decades and later wrote a book about the mission, The Asteroid Hunter."],
g:[["Regolith","Loose material covering a solid surface"],["Nucleobase","A building block of DNA and RNA"],["Touch-and-go","A brief contact to collect a sample"],["Brine","Very salty water"]],
f:["OSIRIS-REx returned 121.6 grams from Bennu.","The Bennu sample contains all five DNA and RNA bases.","The spacecraft will visit Apophis in 2029."],
fun:"Bennu’s surface turned out to be so loose that the spacecraft would have sunk into it if it had not fired its thrusters to back away."});

ADDART({id:"gaia",t:"Gaia Space Observatory",c:"Space Exploration",i:"✨",w:"Gaia (spacecraft)",r:["milky","lagrange","distances","stars","exoplanet"],
p:[["Mapping the galaxy","Gaia made the most precise map of the Milky Way ever."],["Nearly 2 billion stars","It measured positions, distances and motions of about 1.8 billion stars."],["Retired in 2025","Gaia stopped observing in January 2025 and was switched off in March."]],
l:"Gaia was a European Space Agency space observatory that created the largest and most precise three-dimensional map of the Milky Way. Launched on 19 December 2013 and operating from the Sun–Earth L2 Lagrange point about 1.5 million kilometers from Earth, Gaia scanned the whole sky repeatedly, measuring the positions, distances, motions, brightness and colors of nearly 2 billion stars, about 1 percent of the stars in our galaxy. It measured distances using parallax, with accuracy equivalent to measuring the width of a human hair from about 1,000 kilometers away. Gaia’s data releases, especially the third in June 2022, transformed astronomy: they revealed the history of the Milky Way, including ancient mergers with smaller galaxies; mapped how stars move; found hundreds of thousands of asteroids, binary stars and variable stars; and detected dormant black holes. Gaia stopped collecting data on 15 January 2025 and was switched off on 27 March 2025. Its fourth data release, covering the first five and a half years of observations, is planned for 2 December 2026, with a final release around 2030.",
s:[["How Gaia worked","Gaia had two telescopes pointing in different directions, feeding light onto a camera of 106 electronic sensors with nearly a billion pixels, the largest ever sent into space at the time. As the spacecraft spun slowly, stars drifted across the sensors, and their positions were measured many times over years."],["Galactic archaeology","Gaia found that about 8 to 11 billion years ago, the Milky Way swallowed a smaller galaxy, nicknamed Gaia–Enceladus, whose stars still move differently from others. It also showed that the Milky Way’s disk is warped and rippling, possibly from interactions with the Sagittarius dwarf galaxy."],["Black holes","In 2024, astronomers using Gaia data found Gaia BH3, a dormant black hole about 33 times the mass of the Sun only about 1,900 light-years away, the most massive stellar black hole known in our galaxy, detected by the wobble of its companion star."],["Data releases","Gaia’s data are freely available. Data Release 3 included about 1.8 billion sources. Data Release 4, due on 2 December 2026, will include time-series measurements and is expected to reveal thousands of exoplanets through their stars’ wobbles."]],
k:[["Agency","European Space Agency"],["Launch","19 December 2013"],["Location","Sun–Earth L2"],["Stars measured","About 1.8 billion"],["Retired","27 March 2025"]],
d:[["2013","Gaia launches"],["2016","First data release"],["2018","Second data release"],["2022","Third data release"],["2025","Gaia stops observing and is switched off"],["2026","Fourth data release planned (2 December)"]],
s2:[["A gentle goodbye","Before switching Gaia off, controllers moved it away from L2 into a stable orbit around the Sun so it would not interfere with other telescopes, and the team uploaded a farewell message including the names of team members."],["Hipparcos","Gaia succeeded ESA’s Hipparcos mission (1989–1993), the first space mission to measure star positions precisely, which mapped about 118,000 stars. Gaia measured more than 10,000 times as many."]],
sp:["Gaia and asteroids","Although designed to study stars, Gaia also measured the orbits of hundreds of thousands of asteroids in our solar system with great precision, helping planetary defense."],
g:[["Parallax","The apparent shift of a star seen from different places"],["Astrometry","Measuring the positions and motions of stars"],["Data release","A published set of mission data"],["Dormant black hole","A black hole not actively feeding"]],
f:["Gaia mapped about 1.8 billion stars.","Gaia was switched off in March 2025.","Its fourth data release is planned for December 2026."],
fun:"Gaia’s measurements were so precise that it could detect the motion of stars equivalent to the growth of a human hair seen from across a continent."});

ADDART({id:"chandra",t:"Chandra X-ray Observatory",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔭",w:"Chandra X-ray Observatory",r:["scope","bh","supernova","hubblest","spaceshuttle","galaxyclusters","bulletcluster","comacluster","perseuscluster"],
p:[["X-ray eyes","Chandra observes the universe in X-rays, which Earth’s atmosphere blocks."],["Launched by the Shuttle","It was launched in 1999 on a mission commanded by Eileen Collins."],["Fighting budget cuts","Its future has been threatened by NASA budget cuts."]],
l:"The Chandra X-ray Observatory is a NASA space telescope that detects X-rays from very hot and energetic objects in the universe, such as black holes, exploding stars, neutron stars and galaxy clusters. Because Earth’s atmosphere absorbs X-rays, such telescopes must operate in space. Chandra was launched on 23 July 1999 aboard the Space Shuttle Columbia on a mission commanded by Eileen Collins, the first woman to command a Space Shuttle flight. It travels in a long, elliptical orbit reaching about a third of the way to the Moon. Named after Indian-American astrophysicist Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, who won the 1983 Nobel Prize in Physics for his work on how stars die, Chandra is one of NASA’s four ‘Great Observatories’, along with Hubble, the Compton Gamma Ray Observatory and Spitzer. Its mirrors are the smoothest ever made, allowing exceptionally sharp X-ray images. Designed for five years, Chandra has operated for more than 27 years. Proposed NASA budget cuts since 2024 have threatened it; Congress provided at least 63 million dollars for 2026, and astronomers continue to campaign to keep it running.",
s:[["How X-ray telescopes work","X-rays pass through ordinary mirrors, so Chandra uses nested, barrel-shaped mirrors that X-rays strike at shallow angles, like stones skipping on water. The mirrors are polished so smooth that, if the state of Colorado were as smooth, Pikes Peak would be less than an inch tall."],["Discoveries","Chandra has imaged the remains of exploding stars such as Cassiopeia A, discovered powerful jets and outbursts from supermassive black holes, studied the Milky Way’s central black hole, Sagittarius A*, and helped provide evidence for dark matter by observing the Bullet Cluster, where colliding galaxy clusters separated hot gas from mass."],["Images","Chandra images are often combined with images from Hubble, Webb and other telescopes, assigning colors to X-ray data, to create striking pictures of nebulae and galaxies. In 2020, NASA released ‘sonifications’ turning Chandra data into sound."],["Budget threats","In 2024, NASA proposed sharply reducing Chandra’s budget, which scientists warned could end the mission. A 2026 budget line kept it operating at reduced levels. One of its instruments has had problems since October 2025, but Chandra otherwise works normally."]],
k:[["Observes","X-rays"],["Launch","23 July 1999 (Space Shuttle Columbia)"],["Named after","Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar"],["Orbit","Elliptical, up to about a third of the way to the Moon"],["Design life","5 years (now more than 27)"]],
d:[["1976","Chandra proposed to NASA"],["1983","Chandrasekhar wins the Nobel Prize"],["1999","Chandra launched on Columbia"],["2006","Bullet Cluster observations support dark matter"],["2024","Proposed budget cuts threaten Chandra"],["2026","Congress funds Chandra at reduced levels"]],
s2:[["Eileen Collins","Eileen Collins, a US Air Force pilot, became the first woman to pilot a Space Shuttle in 1995 and the first to command one on the Chandra launch mission in 1999. She later commanded the first shuttle flight after the Columbia disaster in 2005."],["The Great Observatories","NASA’s four Great Observatories each studied different kinds of light: Hubble (visible and ultraviolet), Compton (gamma rays), Chandra (X-rays) and Spitzer (infrared). Only Hubble and Chandra still operate."]],
sp:["Chandrasekhar","Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar calculated as a young man in 1930 that a white dwarf star more than about 1.4 times the Sun’s mass cannot support itself and must collapse, a result called the Chandrasekhar limit. His name means ‘moon-holder’ in Sanskrit."],
g:[["X-ray","High-energy light invisible to the eye"],["Great Observatories","NASA’s four large space telescopes"],["Galaxy cluster","A group of hundreds or thousands of galaxies"],["Sonification","Turning data into sound"]],
f:["Earth’s atmosphere blocks X-rays.","Chandra’s mirrors are the smoothest ever made.","Chandra has worked for more than 27 years."],
fun:"Chandra is so sensitive that it could read a stop sign from about 20 kilometers away, in X-ray terms."});

ADDART({id:"skylab",t:"Skylab",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Skylab",r:["iss","mir","saturnv","spacewalks","apollo"],
p:[["America’s first space station","Skylab was the first US space station, launched in 1973."],["Saved by its crew","Astronauts repaired serious damage from its launch."],["A fiery end","Skylab fell back to Earth over Australia in 1979."]],
l:"Skylab was the first space station of the United States, launched on 14 May 1973 on the last flight of a Saturn V rocket. Built from the converted third stage of a Saturn V, it was the largest spacecraft of its time, with a workshop, living quarters, a solar observatory called the Apollo Telescope Mount and an airlock. Skylab was damaged during launch when its micrometeoroid shield and one main solar panel tore away, causing it to overheat. The first crew, launched on 25 May 1973, deployed a parasol-like sunshade and freed the remaining stuck solar panel during spacewalks, saving the station. Three crews of three astronauts lived aboard for 28, 59 and 84 days between 1973 and 1974, conducting medical experiments on long-term weightlessness, observing the Sun, studying Earth and testing living in space. The final crew’s 84-day mission was a US record for 20 years. Skylab was left empty, and plans to boost it with the Space Shuttle came too late; on 11 July 1979 it reentered the atmosphere, scattering debris across the Indian Ocean and Western Australia.",
s:[["Living in space","Skylab had a shower, a dining table, sleeping compartments and an exercise bicycle. Astronauts found that they grew taller in weightlessness and lost muscle and bone. They ran around the inside of a large tank-shaped room for exercise and fun."],["Solar science","The Apollo Telescope Mount took more than 150,000 images of the Sun in ultraviolet and X-rays, revealing coronal holes, regions where the solar wind streams out, and recording solar flares in unprecedented detail."],["The ‘mutiny’","The third crew complained of an exhausting schedule and took an unscheduled rest day in December 1973, sometimes called a strike or mutiny. NASA adjusted their schedule, and the episode taught planners about workloads on long missions."],["Skylab falls","As Skylab’s orbit decayed faster than expected because of high solar activity, the world watched its reentry. Debris fell near Esperance, Western Australia. The local council jokingly fined NASA 400 Australian dollars for littering; a US radio host paid the fine in 2009."]],
k:[["Launch","14 May 1973"],["Crews","Three (1973–1974)"],["Longest mission","84 days"],["Built from","Saturn V third stage"],["Reentry","11 July 1979"]],
d:[["1973","Skylab launches and is damaged"],["1973","First crew repairs the station"],["1974","Third crew completes an 84-day mission"],["1979","Skylab falls to Earth over Australia"],["2009","Esperance’s littering fine is paid"]],
s2:[["Spiders in space","Two spiders, Arabella and Anita, flew on Skylab as part of a student experiment. At first they spun messy webs in weightlessness but soon adapted and spun more normal webs."],["Skylab’s legacy","Skylab’s experiments on health, exercise and living in space informed later stations such as Mir and the International Space Station."]],
sp:["Comet Kohoutek","The third Skylab crew observed Comet Kohoutek in late 1973, which had been predicted to be spectacular but proved disappointing from the ground. The astronauts saw it well from space."],
g:[["Space station","A spacecraft where crews live for long periods"],["Micrometeoroid","A tiny particle of space rock"],["Reentry","Returning into Earth’s atmosphere"],["Coronal hole","A dark region of the Sun’s corona"]],
f:["Skylab was America’s first space station.","Astronauts repaired Skylab with a sunshade.","Skylab fell to Earth over Australia in 1979."],
fun:"A teenager in Esperance found pieces of Skylab and flew to San Francisco to claim a 10,000-dollar prize offered by a newspaper for the first piece delivered."});

ADDART({id:"projectmercury",t:"Project Mercury and Gemini",c:"Space Exploration",i:"👨‍🚀",w:"Project Mercury",r:["space","apollo","gagarin","spacewalks","astronauts"],
p:[["America’s first astronauts","Project Mercury put the first Americans into space."],["The Mercury Seven","NASA’s first seven astronauts became national heroes."],["Practicing for the Moon","Gemini tested spacewalks, docking and long flights for Apollo."]],
l:"Project Mercury (1958–1963) was the first human spaceflight program of the United States, and Project Gemini (1961–1966) was its successor, preparing for the Apollo Moon landings. NASA, founded in 1958, chose seven military test pilots as its first astronauts, the ‘Mercury Seven’, introduced in April 1959. Mercury’s one-person capsules launched on Redstone and Atlas rockets. On 5 May 1961, less than a month after Yuri Gagarin’s orbital flight, Alan Shepard became the first American in space on a 15-minute suborbital flight, and on 20 February 1962, John Glenn became the first American to orbit Earth, circling it three times. Mercury flew six crewed missions. Gemini used larger two-person capsules launched on Titan rockets and flew ten crewed missions in 1965 and 1966. Gemini astronauts made the first US spacewalk, stayed in space for up to two weeks, carried out the first rendezvous between two spacecraft and the first docking, and practiced the skills needed for lunar missions. Gemini 8, in March 1966, nearly ended in disaster when a stuck thruster sent the spacecraft spinning, but Neil Armstrong regained control.",
s:[["The Mercury Seven","The Mercury Seven were Scott Carpenter, Gordon Cooper, John Glenn, Gus Grissom, Wally Schirra, Alan Shepard and Deke Slayton. Their story was told in Tom Wolfe’s book The Right Stuff (1979) and its 1983 film."],["Glenn’s flight","During John Glenn’s Friendship 7 flight, a sensor suggested his heat shield might be loose. Controllers told him to keep his retrorocket pack on during reentry, and he returned safely. In 1998, at age 77, Glenn flew again on the Space Shuttle, becoming the oldest person in space at that time."],["Gemini’s achievements","Ed White made the first American spacewalk on Gemini 4 in June 1965. Gemini 7 spent nearly 14 days in space, and Gemini 6A rendezvoused with it in December 1965. Gemini 8 achieved the first docking in orbit with an uncrewed Agena target vehicle."],["Hidden Figures","African American mathematicians at NASA, including Katherine Johnson, calculated trajectories for Mercury flights. Glenn reportedly asked for Johnson to check the computer’s calculations before his flight. Their story was told in the 2016 book and film Hidden Figures."]],
k:[["Mercury years","1958–1963"],["Gemini years","1961–1966 (flights 1965–1966)"],["First American in space","Alan Shepard, 5 May 1961"],["First American in orbit","John Glenn, 20 February 1962"],["First docking","Gemini 8, 16 March 1966"]],
d:[["1958","NASA founded; Project Mercury begins"],["1959","Mercury Seven introduced"],["1961","Alan Shepard flies into space"],["1962","John Glenn orbits Earth"],["1965","First US spacewalk on Gemini 4"],["1966","First docking on Gemini 8"]],
s2:[["Ham the chimpanzee","Before Shepard’s flight, a chimpanzee named Ham flew a Mercury capsule on 31 January 1961, pulling levers in response to lights, showing that tasks could be performed in spaceflight."],["Liberty Bell 7","Gus Grissom’s capsule, Liberty Bell 7, sank after splashdown in July 1961 when its hatch blew open early. Grissom nearly drowned. The capsule was recovered from the Atlantic seabed in 1999."]],
sp:["Gemini’s name","Gemini, Latin for ‘twins’, was named after the constellation because each capsule carried two astronauts."],
g:[["Suborbital","Reaching space without completing an orbit"],["Rendezvous","Two spacecraft meeting in orbit"],["Docking","Joining two spacecraft"],["Retrorocket","A rocket fired to slow down"]],
f:["Alan Shepard was the first American in space.","John Glenn orbited Earth in 1962.","Gemini made the first docking in space."],
fun:"Alan Shepard’s first flight lasted only about 15 minutes, while John Glenn flew in space again 36 years after his first orbital flight."});

ADDART({id:"soyuz",t:"Soyuz",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Soyuz (spacecraft)",r:["iss","sputnik","spaceports","gagarin","mir"],
p:[["The space workhorse","Soyuz spacecraft have carried people to space since 1967."],["Longest-serving","Soyuz is the longest-used crewed spacecraft in history."],["A lifeline to the ISS","For years it was the only way to reach the International Space Station."]],
l:"Soyuz, meaning ‘union’ in Russian, is a series of spacecraft designed by the Soviet Union in the 1960s and still used by Russia to carry cosmonauts and astronauts to space, making it the longest-serving crewed spacecraft in history. The name also refers to the family of rockets that launch it, descended from the R-7 missile that launched Sputnik in 1957. A Soyuz spacecraft has three parts: a spherical orbital module for living space, a bell-shaped descent module in which the crew of up to three return to Earth, and a service module with engines and solar panels. The first crewed flight, Soyuz 1, ended in tragedy in April 1967 when its parachute failed and cosmonaut Vladimir Komarov was killed; in 1971 the three-man Soyuz 11 crew died when their capsule lost air during return. Since then, Soyuz has become known for reliability. After the Space Shuttle retired in 2011, Soyuz was the only way to carry people to the International Space Station until SpaceX’s Crew Dragon began flights in 2020. Soyuz launches from Baikonur, Kazakhstan, where a launch pad damaged in November 2025 was repaired in March 2026.",
s:[["The spacecraft","The descent module lands on land in the steppes of Kazakhstan under a large parachute, firing small rockets about a meter above the ground to soften the landing. Crews describe the landing as a hard jolt. The other modules burn up in the atmosphere."],["Soyuz and the ISS","Soyuz spacecraft dock with the ISS and remain attached as lifeboats during crews’ stays of about six months. Under a seat-swap agreement, American astronauts fly on Soyuz and Russian cosmonauts on US commercial spacecraft."],["Emergencies","On 11 October 2018, the Soyuz MS-10 rocket failed shortly after launch. The launch escape system pulled the capsule away, and astronauts Aleksey Ovchinin and Nick Hague landed safely. Both flew again months later. In 1983, Soyuz T-10-1 crew escaped seconds before their rocket exploded on the pad."],["Baikonur pad damage","After Soyuz MS-28 launched on 27 November 2025, a service platform collapsed into the flame trench at Baikonur’s Site 31, the only pad able to launch crews to the ISS. Russia repaired the pad, and launches resumed in March 2026."]],
k:[["Meaning","‘Union’"],["First crewed flight","Soyuz 1, April 1967"],["Crew","Up to three"],["Landing","On land in Kazakhstan"],["Launch site","Baikonur Cosmodrome"]],
d:[["1967","Soyuz 1 disaster kills Vladimir Komarov"],["1971","Soyuz 11 crew dies during return"],["1975","Apollo–Soyuz docking in orbit"],["2011","Soyuz becomes the only crew vehicle to the ISS"],["2018","Soyuz MS-10 crew survives a launch failure"],["2025","Launch pad damaged after Soyuz MS-28"],["2026","Pad repaired; launches resume"]],
s2:[["Apollo–Soyuz","In July 1975, an American Apollo spacecraft docked with Soyuz 19 in orbit, and the crews shook hands in space, a symbol of détente during the Cold War and a forerunner of later cooperation on the ISS."],["Space tourists","Between 2001 and 2009, seven private space tourists, including Dennis Tito, flew to the ISS aboard Soyuz, paying tens of millions of dollars each."]],
sp:["Traditions","Before launch, Soyuz crews follow traditions, such as watching the film White Sun of the Desert, signing their hotel room doors and planting trees at Baikonur. A small toy hangs in the capsule as a zero-gravity indicator."],
g:[["Descent module","The capsule that returns the crew to Earth"],["Launch escape system","A rocket that pulls the capsule away in an emergency"],["Steppe","A large, flat grassland"],["Détente","An easing of tension between countries"]],
f:["Soyuz has flown people since 1967.","Soyuz capsules land on land, not water.","Soyuz was the only way to the ISS from 2011 to 2020."],
fun:"Soyuz crews carry a small soft toy that starts floating when the spacecraft reaches weightlessness, a tradition started by Yuri Gagarin’s doll."});

ADDART({id:"saturnv",t:"Saturn V",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Saturn V",r:["apollo","rocket","skylab","starship","space"],
p:[["The Moon rocket","Saturn V launched the Apollo missions to the Moon."],["A giant","At 110 meters tall, it was taller than the Statue of Liberty."],["Never lost a payload","All 13 Saturn V launches delivered their payloads."]],
l:"Saturn V was the giant three-stage rocket that carried American astronauts to the Moon in the Apollo program. Developed by NASA’s Marshall Space Flight Center under Wernher von Braun, it stood 110.6 meters tall and weighed about 2.9 million kilograms fully fueled. Its first stage, powered by five F-1 engines burning kerosene and liquid oxygen, produced about 35 million newtons of thrust, burning through more than 2 million kilograms of propellant in about two and a half minutes. The second and third stages burned liquid hydrogen. Saturn V first flew uncrewed on Apollo 4 on 9 November 1967; Apollo 8 in December 1968 used it to carry the first humans around the Moon, and Apollo 11 launched on a Saturn V on 16 July 1969. Thirteen Saturn Vs were launched between 1967 and 1973, the last carrying the Skylab space station, and none lost its payload. It remained the most powerful rocket successfully flown until NASA’s Space Launch System in 2022 and SpaceX’s Starship. Three unused Saturn Vs are displayed in Florida, Texas and Alabama.",
s:[["The F-1 engine","The F-1 is still the most powerful single-chamber liquid-fueled rocket engine ever flown. Engineers struggled with violent combustion instability, which they solved by setting off small bombs inside test engines and redesigning the injectors."],["Three stages","The first stage lifted the rocket to about 67 kilometers. The second stage took it nearly to orbit, and the third stage completed the orbit and later fired again to send Apollo toward the Moon. At the top sat the Apollo spacecraft and a launch escape tower."],["Building it","Saturn V was assembled in the Vehicle Assembly Building at Kennedy Space Center, one of the largest buildings in the world by volume, and carried to the launch pad on a giant crawler-transporter moving at about 1.6 kilometers per hour."],["Apollo 6 and Apollo 13","On Apollo 6, an uncrewed test, violent ‘pogo’ vibrations and engine failures nearly ended the flight, but engineers fixed the problems before crews flew. On Apollo 13, the second stage’s center engine shut down early, but the rocket still reached orbit."]],
k:[["Height","110.6 m"],["Fueled weight","About 2.9 million kg"],["First-stage thrust","About 35 million newtons"],["Launches","13 (1967–1973)"],["Chief designer","Wernher von Braun"]],
d:[["1962","Saturn V design chosen"],["1967","First launch, Apollo 4"],["1968","Apollo 8 carries the first crew to the Moon"],["1969","Apollo 11 launches on a Saturn V"],["1973","Final Saturn V launches Skylab"]],
s2:[["Sound of power","The Saturn V launch was so loud that sound waves could be felt kilometers away; NASA placed spectators at least 5 kilometers from the pad. Water was not yet used to suppress sound as on later rockets."],["Von Braun","Wernher von Braun had designed the V-2 missile for Nazi Germany, built using forced labor, before being brought to the United States after World War II. His role in the space program remains admired for its achievements and criticized for his wartime past."]],
sp:["F-1 engines found","In 2013, an expedition funded by Amazon founder Jeff Bezos recovered parts of F-1 engines from Apollo missions from the Atlantic seabed, about 4 kilometers deep, including pieces from Apollo 11."],
g:[["Stage","A section of a rocket with its own engines"],["Thrust","The force that pushes a rocket"],["Combustion instability","Dangerous vibrations inside a rocket engine"],["Crawler-transporter","A giant vehicle that moves rockets to the pad"]],
f:["Saturn V was 110.6 meters tall.","All 13 Saturn Vs delivered their payloads.","The F-1 is the most powerful single-chamber engine flown."],
fun:"Each of the Saturn V’s five F-1 engines pumped about 2.5 metric tons of propellant per second."});

ADDART({id:"apollo13",t:"Apollo 13",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌘",w:"Apollo 13",r:["apollo","saturnv","moon","fuelcells","artemis","artemisii"],
p:[["‘A successful failure’","Apollo 13’s Moon landing was cancelled after an explosion, but the crew returned safely."],["‘Houston, we’ve had a problem’","Astronaut Jack Swigert reported the emergency on 13 April 1970."],["A lifeboat in space","The lunar module kept the astronauts alive on the way home."]],
l:"Apollo 13 was the third NASA mission planned to land on the Moon, launched on 11 April 1970 with astronauts Jim Lovell, Jack Swigert and Fred Haise. On 13 April, about 56 hours into the flight and more than 300,000 kilometers from Earth, an oxygen tank in the service module exploded, damaging another tank and crippling the command module’s supply of power, oxygen and water. Swigert radioed, ‘Okay, Houston, we’ve had a problem here.’ The Moon landing was abandoned, and the crew moved into the lunar module, Aquarius, designed to support two people for two days, using it as a lifeboat for four days. With mission control engineers, the crew swung around the Moon and fired the lunar module’s engine to speed their return. They endured cold, little water and rising carbon dioxide, solved by building an adapter from cardboard, plastic bags and tape to fit square air filters into round sockets. On 17 April 1970 they splashed down safely in the Pacific. Apollo 13 set a record for the farthest humans from Earth, which stood until Artemis II in April 2026.",
s:[["The cause","Investigators found that a thermostat switch in oxygen tank 2 had not been upgraded to handle higher voltage, and during ground testing it welded shut, letting heaters overheat and damage wire insulation. In flight, when the crew stirred the tank, a spark ignited the insulation in pure oxygen, causing the explosion."],["The ‘mailbox’","Carbon dioxide built up because the lunar module’s round filter canisters could not handle three people for so long, and the command module’s square canisters did not fit. Engineers on the ground designed a fix from items on board, and the astronauts built it, nicknamed the ‘mailbox’."],["Getting home","Controllers led by flight director Gene Kranz worked out new procedures to power up the cold command module for reentry using limited battery power. The crew separated from the lunar module, nicknamed ‘lifeboat’, shortly before reentry and splashed down near Samoa."],["Records","Swinging around the far side of the Moon, Apollo 13 reached about 400,171 kilometers from Earth, the farthest any humans had traveled. Artemis II broke the record on 6 April 2026, reaching about 406,771 kilometers."]],
k:[["Launch","11 April 1970"],["Crew","Jim Lovell, Jack Swigert, Fred Haise"],["Explosion","13 April 1970"],["Lifeboat","Lunar module Aquarius"],["Splashdown","17 April 1970"]],
d:[["1970","Apollo 13 launches (11 April)"],["1970","Oxygen tank explodes (13 April)"],["1970","Crew splashes down safely (17 April)"],["1995","Film Apollo 13 released"],["2026","Artemis II breaks Apollo 13’s distance record"]],
s2:[["The film","Ron Howard’s 1995 film Apollo 13, starring Tom Hanks as Jim Lovell, made the mission famous to new generations. Its line ‘Houston, we have a problem’ slightly changed the real words."],["Ken Mattingly","Ken Mattingly was replaced on the crew three days before launch because he had been exposed to German measles. He never caught it and helped develop power-up procedures on the ground. He later flew on Apollo 16."]],
sp:["‘Failure is not an option’","The phrase ‘Failure is not an option’, associated with flight director Gene Kranz, was actually written for the 1995 film, though it captured the attitude of mission control. Kranz later used it as the title of his memoir."],
g:[["Service module","The part of Apollo with engines, oxygen and power"],["Command module","The capsule where the crew lived and returned"],["Lunar module","The spacecraft designed to land on the Moon"],["Carbon dioxide","A gas breathed out that is harmful in large amounts"]],
f:["Apollo 13 was called a ‘successful failure’.","The lunar module served as a lifeboat.","Artemis II broke Apollo 13’s distance record in 2026."],
fun:"After splashdown, the lunar module’s maker, Grumman, jokingly sent North American Rockwell, the maker of the damaged service module, an invoice for ‘towing’ the spacecraft back to Earth."});

ADDART({id:"spaceanimals",t:"Animals in Space",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🐕",w:"Animals in space",r:["sputnik","space","tardigrades","projectmercury","animaltesting"],
p:[["Pioneers before people","Animals flew into space before humans to test safety."],["Laika","The dog Laika was the first animal to orbit Earth, in 1957."],["Many species","Flies, monkeys, dogs, cats, mice, fish and tardigrades have flown."]],
l:"Animals were sent into space before humans to test whether living things could survive launch, weightlessness and radiation, and they are still used in research. The first animals in space were fruit flies, launched by the United States aboard a captured German V-2 rocket on 20 February 1947; they returned alive. Monkeys followed, beginning with Albert II in 1949, though many early animal flights ended in death. The Soviet Union used stray dogs, believing they were tough. On 3 November 1957, the dog Laika became the first animal to orbit Earth aboard Sputnik 2, but she died within hours from overheating, as there was no plan to return her. On 19 August 1960, the dogs Belka and Strelka orbited Earth and returned safely. In January 1961, the chimpanzee Ham flew on a US Mercury capsule, and in 1963 France launched a cat, Félicette, into space. In 1968, tortoises aboard the Soviet Zond 5 became the first animals to travel around the Moon. Animal flights raised ethical concerns, and many countries honor these animals with monuments.",
s:[["Soviet space dogs","The Soviet Union flew dozens of dogs, mostly female strays weighing about 6 kilograms, on suborbital and orbital flights in the 1950s and 1960s. They were trained in small capsules and centrifuges. Belka and Strelka’s survival paved the way for Yuri Gagarin’s flight in April 1961."],["Laika","Laika, a mixed-breed stray from Moscow, became world-famous. The Soviet government claimed for decades that she lived for days, but in 2002 it was revealed that she died from overheating within hours. A monument to her was unveiled in Moscow in 2008."],["Monkeys and chimpanzees","The United States flew rhesus monkeys and chimpanzees. Ham, trained to pull levers in response to lights, flew on 31 January 1961 and survived. Enos orbited Earth in November 1961, before John Glenn."],["Animals today","Mice, fish, worms, spiders, jellyfish and plants have flown on space stations to study how weightlessness affects bones, muscles, development and behavior. Tardigrades, tiny water bears, survived exposure to open space in 2007."]],
k:[["First animals in space","Fruit flies, 1947"],["First animal in orbit","Laika, 3 November 1957"],["First to orbit and return","Belka and Strelka, 1960"],["First cat","Félicette, 1963"],["First around the Moon","Tortoises on Zond 5, 1968"]],
d:[["1947","Fruit flies reach space and return"],["1949","Albert II monkey reaches space"],["1957","Laika orbits Earth on Sputnik 2"],["1960","Belka and Strelka orbit and return"],["1961","Ham the chimpanzee flies on Mercury"],["1963","Félicette the cat flies"],["1968","Tortoises fly around the Moon"]],
s2:[["A gift from space","One of Strelka’s puppies, Pushinka, was given by Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev to the daughter of US President John F. Kennedy in 1961. Pushinka had puppies with a Kennedy family dog."],["Félicette","Félicette, a stray from Paris, flew to about 157 kilometers on 18 October 1963 and returned alive, but was later euthanized for study. A statue honoring her was unveiled in Strasbourg in 2019 after a crowdfunding campaign."]],
sp:["Tortoises around the Moon","In September 1968, two Russian tortoises aboard Zond 5 flew around the Moon and returned to Earth alive, along with flies and worms, three months before the Apollo 8 astronauts."],
g:[["Suborbital","Reaching space without orbiting"],["Centrifuge","A spinning machine that simulates strong forces"],["Weightlessness","Floating in free fall"],["Euthanize","To end an animal’s life painlessly"]],
f:["Fruit flies were the first animals in space.","Laika was the first animal to orbit Earth.","Tortoises flew around the Moon before humans."],
fun:"Before Apollo 8’s astronauts, the first living things to circle the Moon were two tortoises, which lost some weight but otherwise returned healthy."});

ADDART({id:"magnetars",t:"Magnetars",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🧲",w:"Magnetar",r:["pulsar","supernova","gammaraybursts","magnet","radioastronomy"],
p:[["The strongest magnets","Magnetars are neutron stars with the strongest magnetic fields known."],["Starquakes","Cracks in their crusts release huge bursts of energy."],["Fast radio bursts","A magnetar was linked to a mysterious fast radio burst in 2020."]],
l:"A magnetar is a type of neutron star, the ultra-dense remnant of a massive star that exploded as a supernova, with an extraordinarily powerful magnetic field, about a thousand times stronger than an ordinary neutron star’s and roughly a quadrillion times stronger than Earth’s. Magnetars are only about 20 kilometers across but contain more mass than the Sun. Their magnetic fields are so intense that they would distort atoms and erase the data on credit cards from halfway to the Moon. As the field twists and decays, it stresses the star’s solid crust, causing ‘starquakes’ that release bursts of X-rays and gamma rays. Magnetars were first identified after a burst of gamma rays on 5 March 1979 was traced to a supernova remnant in the Large Magellanic Cloud. On 27 December 2004, a giant flare from the magnetar SGR 1806-20, about 30,000 light-years away, was so powerful that it briefly affected Earth’s upper atmosphere. About 30 magnetars are known. In 2020, a magnetar in our galaxy produced a fast radio burst, solving part of a long-standing mystery.",
s:[["How magnetars form","When a massive star collapses, its core may become a neutron star. If the core spins very rapidly, perhaps hundreds of times a second, it may generate a magnetic field through a dynamo effect far stronger than usual. Magnetars slow down quickly and stay active for only about 10,000 years."],["Giant flares","Only a few giant flares have been seen. The 2004 flare from SGR 1806-20 released more energy in a tenth of a second than the Sun emits in about 100,000 years, and its gamma rays briefly changed the ionosphere, affecting radio communications."],["Fast radio bursts","Fast radio bursts are extremely brief, powerful flashes of radio waves, mostly from distant galaxies. On 28 April 2020, telescopes detected one from SGR 1935+2154 in the Milky Way at the same time as an X-ray burst, showing that magnetars can produce them."],["Extreme physics","Magnetars’ fields are so strong that, near the surface, light can split into two or merge, and atoms would be stretched into thin needle shapes. Scientists study them to test physics under conditions impossible on Earth."]],
k:[["Type","Neutron star"],["Size","About 20 km across"],["Magnetic field","About a quadrillion times Earth’s"],["Known magnetars","About 30"],["Famous giant flare","SGR 1806-20, 27 December 2004"]],
d:[["1979","Gamma-ray burst from the Large Magellanic Cloud detected"],["1992","Magnetar theory proposed by Robert Duncan and Christopher Thompson"],["1998","Magnetar’s field measured, confirming the theory"],["2004","Giant flare from SGR 1806-20"],["2020","Magnetar produces a fast radio burst"]],
s2:[["Too dangerous to approach","Within about 1,000 kilometers of a magnetar, its magnetic field would kill a person by distorting the atoms in their body. Fortunately, the nearest known magnetars are thousands of light-years away."],["The 1979 event","The burst of 5 March 1979 was detected by nine spacecraft across the solar system, allowing astronomers to pinpoint its source in the remnant N49. It was far brighter than any burst seen before."]],
sp:["Duncan and Thompson","Astrophysicists Robert Duncan and Christopher Thompson proposed magnetars in 1992 to explain unusual repeating gamma-ray bursts. Their idea was confirmed in 1998 by measurements of a magnetar’s slowing spin."],
g:[["Neutron star","The collapsed core of a massive star"],["Starquake","A sudden shift in a neutron star’s crust"],["Fast radio burst","A millisecond flash of radio waves"],["Ionosphere","A charged layer of Earth’s upper atmosphere"]],
f:["Magnetars have the strongest magnetic fields known.","A magnetar flare affected Earth’s atmosphere in 2004.","A magnetar produced a fast radio burst in 2020."],
fun:"A teaspoon of material from a neutron star like a magnetar would weigh about a billion metric tons on Earth."});

ADDART({id:"lensing",t:"Gravitational Lensing",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔍",w:"Gravitational lens",r:["relat","einstein","darkmatter","jwst","eclipse","galaxyclusters","bulletcluster","rogueplanets"],
p:[["Gravity bends light","Massive objects bend light passing near them, like a lens."],["Proof for Einstein","The 1919 eclipse confirmed that the Sun bends starlight."],["A cosmic magnifying glass","Lensing reveals distant galaxies, dark matter and planets."]],
l:"Gravitational lensing is the bending of light by the gravity of a massive object, such as a galaxy, a cluster of galaxies, a star or a black hole, which acts like a lens. According to Albert Einstein’s general theory of relativity, published in 1915, mass curves space-time, and light follows that curve. Einstein predicted that the Sun would bend starlight by a small amount; British astronomer Arthur Eddington’s expedition to observe the total solar eclipse of 29 May 1919 measured this effect, making Einstein world-famous. Lensing can produce multiple images of the same distant object, arcs and complete circles called Einstein rings. The first lensed object, the ‘Twin Quasar’, was discovered in 1979. Strong lensing by galaxy clusters magnifies faint galaxies behind them, letting telescopes such as the James Webb Space Telescope see some of the earliest galaxies. Weak lensing, slight distortions of the shapes of many galaxies, maps invisible dark matter. Microlensing, the brightening of a star when another passes in front of it, has revealed planets and isolated black holes.",
s:[["Types of lensing","In strong lensing, a massive galaxy or cluster creates obvious multiple images, arcs or rings. In weak lensing, distortions are tiny and must be measured statistically over millions of galaxies. In microlensing, the lens is a star or planet, causing a temporary brightening rather than visible distortion."],["The 1919 eclipse","During a total eclipse, stars near the Sun become visible. Eddington and colleagues photographed stars from Príncipe, an island off Africa, and Sobral, Brazil. The stars appeared shifted by an amount matching Einstein’s prediction, twice the amount predicted by Newtonian physics."],["Mapping dark matter","Because lensing depends on all mass, including invisible dark matter, astronomers use it to map dark matter around galaxy clusters. ESA’s Euclid telescope, launched in 2023, is mapping weak lensing across a third of the sky to study dark matter and dark energy."],["Magnified views","JWST’s first deep field image in July 2022 showed the galaxy cluster SMACS 0723 bending light from galaxies far behind it into arcs. Lensing has magnified individual stars billions of light-years away, such as Earendel, seen as it was about 900 million years after the Big Bang."]],
k:[["Cause","Gravity bending light"],["Predicted by","Albert Einstein (general relativity, 1915)"],["First confirmed","1919 solar eclipse"],["First lensed quasar","Twin Quasar, 1979"],["Uses","Distant galaxies, dark matter, planets"]],
d:[["1915","Einstein publishes general relativity"],["1919","Eclipse confirms light bending"],["1936","Einstein publishes on lensing by stars"],["1979","Twin Quasar discovered"],["2022","JWST’s first deep field shows lensed galaxies"],["2023","Euclid launches to map lensing"]],
s2:[["Einstein rings and crosses","When a source, lens and observer line up exactly, the source appears as a ring around the lens, an Einstein ring. The ‘Einstein Cross’ is a quasar seen as four images around a foreground galaxy."],["Seeing a supernova again","In 2014, a supernova behind a galaxy cluster appeared as four images. Astronomers predicted another image would appear about a year later because its light took a longer path, and in 2015 it did, as forecast."]],
sp:["Planets by microlensing","Microlensing can detect planets far from their stars and even free-floating planets with no star. NASA’s Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, planned for launch in the late 2020s, will search for thousands of planets this way."],
g:[["Space-time","Space and time combined in relativity"],["Einstein ring","A ring-shaped image produced by lensing"],["Quasar","A very bright galaxy center powered by a black hole"],["Microlensing","Brightening caused by a star or planet acting as a lens"]],
f:["Gravity can bend light.","The 1919 eclipse confirmed Einstein’s prediction.","Lensing helps map invisible dark matter."],
fun:"Astronomers correctly predicted the date when a lensed supernova would reappear in a new spot in the sky, because its light took a longer path around a galaxy."});

ADDART({id:"interstellarobjects",t:"Interstellar Objects",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌠",w:"Interstellar object",r:["comet","asteroidbelt","exoplanet","scope","fermiparadox"],
p:[["Visitors from other stars","Interstellar objects come from outside our solar system."],["Only three known","‘Oumuamua, Borisov and 3I/ATLAS have been found so far."],["Clues from far away","They carry material from other planetary systems."]],
l:"Interstellar objects are asteroids, comets or other bodies that originate outside the solar system and pass through it on their way between the stars. Astronomers recognize them by their speed and hyperbolic orbits, open paths showing that they are not bound to the Sun. Scientists had long expected them, because planetary systems throw out huge numbers of small bodies as they form, but none was identified until 19 October 2017, when the Pan-STARRS telescope in Hawaii discovered 1I/ʻOumuamua, a strange, elongated or flattened object that tumbled through space without a visible tail and was gently pushed by a force not fully explained. In August 2019, amateur astronomer Gennadiy Borisov found 2I/Borisov, which looked like an ordinary comet but was unusually rich in carbon monoxide. On 1 July 2025, the ATLAS survey telescope in Chile discovered 3I/ATLAS, a large, active comet that may be billions of years older than the solar system. Interstellar objects offer samples of other planetary systems. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory is expected to find many more.",
s:[["ʻOumuamua","ʻOumuamua, a Hawaiian name meaning roughly ‘scout’ or ‘messenger from afar arriving first’, was spotted after it had already passed the Sun. It was dark reddish, perhaps 100 to 400 meters long, and accelerated slightly as it left. Most scientists think gas released from ices, such as hydrogen, caused the push, though one astronomer, Avi Loeb, suggested it might be alien technology, an idea most researchers rejected."],["2I/Borisov","Borisov was found by Gennadiy Borisov using a telescope he built himself in Crimea. It had a fuzzy coma and tail like a typical comet, showing that comets in other planetary systems can be similar to ours, though its high carbon monoxide content suggests it formed in a very cold place."],["3I/ATLAS","3I/ATLAS traveled at about 58 kilometers per second relative to the Sun and came closest to the Sun in late October 2025. Spacecraft at Mars and elsewhere observed it, finding a coma rich in carbon dioxide, nickel and water. Its path suggests it comes from the older ‘thick disk’ of the Milky Way."],["Chasing a visitor","Because interstellar objects move fast and are found late, catching one is difficult. ESA’s Comet Interceptor mission, planned for launch around 2029, will wait in space for a suitable comet, possibly an interstellar one, to visit."]],
k:[["Known objects","Three (as of 2026)"],["First","1I/ʻOumuamua, 2017"],["Second","2I/Borisov, 2019"],["Third","3I/ATLAS, 2025"],["How identified","Hyperbolic orbit and high speed"]],
d:[["2017","ʻOumuamua discovered (19 October)"],["2019","2I/Borisov discovered (30 August)"],["2025","3I/ATLAS discovered (1 July)"],["2025","3I/ATLAS passes closest to the Sun (October)"],["c. 2029","Comet Interceptor planned launch"]],
s2:[["Naming","Interstellar objects receive the letter I and a number in order of discovery: 1I, 2I, 3I. The International Astronomical Union created the category in 2017 for ʻOumuamua."],["How many?","Astronomers estimate that at any time, several interstellar objects the size of ʻOumuamua may be passing within the orbit of Neptune, but most are too faint to detect with current telescopes."]],
sp:["Interstellar meteors?","Some scientists have claimed that small meteors that struck Earth, such as one over Papua New Guinea in 2014, came from interstellar space, but these claims are disputed because of uncertainties in the data."],
g:[["Interstellar","Between the stars"],["Hyperbolic orbit","An open path not bound to the Sun"],["Coma","The cloud of gas and dust around a comet"],["Survey telescope","A telescope that scans large areas of sky"]],
f:["Only three interstellar objects are known.","ʻOumuamua was the first, found in 2017.","3I/ATLAS may be older than the solar system."],
fun:"Gennadiy Borisov discovered the second interstellar object with a 0.65-meter telescope he built himself."});

ADDART({id:"spacebody",t:"The Human Body in Space",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🧑‍🚀",w:"Effect of spaceflight on the human body",r:["astronauts","iss","bones","muscles","cosmicrays"],
p:[["Weightlessness changes the body","In space, bones and muscles weaken and fluids shift upward."],["Radiation risk","Astronauts are exposed to more radiation than on Earth."],["Exercise every day","Astronauts exercise about two hours a day to stay healthy."]],
l:"Spaceflight affects almost every system of the human body, a key challenge for long missions to the Moon and Mars. In weightlessness, without gravity pulling down, body fluids shift toward the head, giving astronauts puffy faces and ‘bird legs’ and affecting their vision. Without weight to support, bones lose about 1 to 1.5 percent of their mass per month in some areas, and muscles weaken; astronauts exercise about two hours every day on treadmills, bicycles and resistance machines. The spine lengthens, making astronauts up to about 5 centimeters taller temporarily. Many feel ‘space sickness’ in the first days as the balance organs in the inner ear adjust. Radiation from the Sun and cosmic rays is much higher than on Earth, increasing long-term cancer risk. Isolation and confinement can affect mood and sleep. NASA’s Twins Study, comparing astronaut Scott Kelly, who spent nearly a year on the ISS in 2015–2016, with his identical twin, Mark, found many changes, most of which returned to normal after landing. The longest single spaceflight was Valeri Polyakov’s 437 days on Mir in 1994–1995.",
s:[["Bones and muscles","Weight-bearing bones such as the hips and spine lose minerals fastest. Without countermeasures, astronauts could lose bone at about ten times the rate of osteoporosis on Earth. Exercise and good nutrition reduce but do not completely prevent losses, and bones may take years to recover."],["Eyes and brain","About 70 percent of astronauts on long missions develop changes in their eyes, such as swelling of the optic nerve and flattening of the eyeball, called spaceflight-associated neuro-ocular syndrome. The brain also shifts slightly upward in the skull."],["Radiation","On the ISS, astronauts receive about 10 times or more the radiation dose people get on Earth in the same time. Beyond Earth’s magnetic field, on trips to Mars, doses would be much higher. Shielding, shorter travel times and medicines are being studied."],["Coming home","After returning, astronauts often feel dizzy and heavy, may struggle to walk and need weeks or months to readapt. Some reported that they tried to let go of objects in midair, forgetting that they would fall."]],
k:[["Bone loss","About 1–1.5% per month in some bones"],["Daily exercise","About 2 hours"],["Height gain","Up to about 5 cm"],["Longest single flight","Valeri Polyakov, 437 days"],["Twins Study","Scott and Mark Kelly"]],
d:[["1961","Gagarin shows humans can survive in space"],["1973–1974","Skylab studies long stays in space"],["1995","Polyakov completes 437 days on Mir"],["2016","Scott Kelly returns from nearly a year in space"],["2019","NASA Twins Study results published"],["2023","Frank Rubio sets a US record of 371 days"]],
s2:[["Space and the immune system","Spaceflight can alter the immune system, and dormant viruses such as those causing cold sores sometimes reactivate in astronauts. Scientists also study how microbes behave aboard spacecraft."],["Artificial gravity","Future spacecraft might spin to create artificial gravity, reducing bone and muscle loss. Experiments with short-arm centrifuges, which spin people lying down, are being tested on Earth."]],
sp:["Sleeping in space","Astronauts sleep in small cabins, zipped into sleeping bags attached to walls so they do not float around. Without a sense of up or down, they can sleep in any orientation, and some report vivid dreams."],
g:[["Weightlessness","Floating in free fall"],["Countermeasure","An action to prevent harm"],["Space sickness","Nausea in the first days in space"],["Osteoporosis","A disease that weakens bones"]],
f:["Astronauts grow up to about 5 cm taller in space.","Astronauts exercise about two hours a day.","The longest spaceflight lasted 437 days."],
fun:"Astronauts’ feet lose their tough skin in space because they rarely walk, while the tops of their feet get calluses from hooking under handrails."});
